# =============================================================================
# lege — Reads input from the active input stream.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • stdin input with `lege` — `read` returns all of stdin to end of input,
#     here the one line of the sibling lege.stdin fixture
#   • the `↦ regex` conversion — compiling a textus pattern into a regex value
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • Not checking `is none` before using the value — `lege` may return `null` at end of input.
#
# See also: lineam, scribe
# =============================================================================

# lege and regex conversio — I/O read and compiled pattern expressions
#
# sit _ pattern ← "\d+" ↦ regex   -- compiled regex from textus
# sit _ input ← lege                  -- stdin line read
#
# GRAMMAR:
#   conversio :← stringLit '↦' 'regex'
#   readExpr :← 'lege'
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   lege.expected — the compiled regex pattern, then the fixture text. The text
#   keeps its own final newline, so `print` leaves a blank line after it.
#
# BACKEND:
#   Targets read the fixture through the case manifest's `stdin` field; the
#   outcomes row of each target pins how it handles a whole-stream read.

main {
    let regex pattern ← "(?i)\\d+" ↦ regex
    print pattern
    let string ∪ none input ← read
    print input
}
